========================================
TRANSITION-TIMING-FUNCTION – COMPLETE NOTES
========================================

Definition:
-----------
transition-timing-function controls how the
speed changes during a transition.

It defines the acceleration curve of the animation.

It does NOT control:
• Duration
• Delay
• Start value
• End value

It controls only the speed pattern between
the start and end values.

--------------------------------------------------

How It Works:
-------------
When a property changes:

Start Value  →  End Value

The browser:
• Creates a timeline (0% → 100%)
• Calculates intermediate values
• Updates the property gradually (frame by frame)

The timing function decides how fast or slow
the property changes during that time.

--------------------------------------------------

Default Value:
--------------
ease

If not specified, the browser automatically
applies 'ease'.

--------------------------------------------------

Syntax:
-------

transition-timing-function: value;

OR (shorthand)

transition: property duration timing-function delay;

Order in shorthand:
transition: property duration timing-function delay;

--------------------------------------------------

Types of Timing Functions:
--------------------------

1) linear
----------
transition-timing-function: linear;

• Constant speed
• No acceleration
• No deceleration
• 50% time = 50% progress

Best for:
• Progress bars
• Continuous mechanical movement

--------------------------------------------------

2) ease (default)
-----------------
transition-timing-function: ease;

• Slow start
• Fast middle
• Slow end
• Natural looking animation

--------------------------------------------------

3) ease-in
-----------
transition-timing-function: ease-in;

• Starts slow
• Speeds up at the end
• Feels like acceleration

--------------------------------------------------

4) ease-out
------------
transition-timing-function: ease-out;

• Starts fast
• Slows down at the end
• Feels like braking

--------------------------------------------------

5) ease-in-out
---------------
transition-timing-function: ease-in-out;

• Slow start
• Fast middle
• Slow end
• Smooth and balanced motion

--------------------------------------------------

6) cubic-bezier()
------------------
transition-timing-function: cubic-bezier(x1, y1, x2, y2);

• Custom speed curve
• Full control over acceleration
• Used for advanced UI animations
• Can create bounce or elastic effects

--------------------------------------------------

7) steps()
-----------
transition-timing-function: steps(n);

Example:
transition-timing-function: steps(4);

• No smooth animation
• Moves in fixed jumps
• Divides transition into equal steps

Used for:
• Sprite animation
• Frame-based effects
• Typing effects

Optional:
steps(4, start)
steps(4, end)

--------------------------------------------------

Important Rule:
---------------
Always define transition on the base element,
not inside :hover.

Wrong:

button:hover {
  transition: 0.5s ease;
}

Correct:

button {
  transition: 0.5s ease;
}

If written inside :hover, the transition
disappears when hover is removed.

--------------------------------------------------

Multiple Timing Functions:
---------------------------
Different properties can use different
timing functions.

Example:

transition:
  width 0.5s ease-in,
  background-color 0.5s linear;

Each property can have:
• Its own duration
• Its own timing-function
• Its own delay

--------------------------------------------------

Professional Usage Guide:
--------------------------
• Button hover → ease-in-out
• Modal open → ease-out
• Slide menu → ease-out
• Progress bar → linear
• Natural UI animations → ease

--------------------------------------------------

Final Summary:
--------------
transition-timing-function defines the
acceleration curve that controls how
intermediate values are calculated
during a CSS transition.

It affects only the speed pattern,
not duration, delay, or values.